      "type": "done",
      "answer": "Adding arbitrarily many processors fails to deliver unlimited speedup for a fixed-size program because of the **serial fraction** of the code. According to Amdahl's Law, even a small portion of the program that must be executed serially (i.e., cannot be parallelized) acts as a bottleneck that caps the total possible speedup.\n\nThe lecture explains this using the formula for speedup $S(p)$:\n\n$$S(p) = \\frac{1}{(1 - f) + \\frac{f}{p}}$$\n\nWhere:\n*   $f$ is the parallelizable fraction of the work.\n*   $1 - f$ is the serial fraction.\n*   $p$ is the number of processors.\n\nAs the number of processors $p$ approaches infinity, the term $\\frac{f}{p}$ approaches zero. However, the term $(1 - f)$ remains constant. Therefore, the maximum speedup is limited to $\\frac{1}{1 - f}$. The lecture illustrates this with numerical examples:\n*   If 90% of the work is parallelizable ($f = 0.90$), the maximum speedup is capped at **10x**.\n*   If 99% of the work is parallelizable ($f = 0.99$), the maximum speedup is capped at **100x**.\n\nThus, no matter how many processors you add, you cannot exceed these limits because the serial portion of the program remains unchanged and must still be executed sequentially. [S1]\n\n**Source:** Page 66 [S1]",
